Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 01:05:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 30/32] n64: the losses per CPU cache line fill read from
 DPC_PIPEBUSY; spans blended without depth measured (rdp_timing v72 / v73)

Two corrections to the table of patch 0029 (what a CPU cache line fill
costs the RDP, by what the RDP is drawing).

1. The values were read from DPC_CLOCK, which on the console keeps
counting until the test ROM's load loop notices the end of the list (up
to one chunk of the load: about 7k clocks for the data loads, 30k for the
instruction load). DPC_PIPEBUSY stops when the RDP does. rdp_timing v72
measured the lists of one kind of work again with DPC_PIPEBUSY recorded:
the values read from DPC_CLOCK repeat those of v63 to v65 within 0.05
clocks; read from DPC_PIPEBUSY a data fill costs 0.03 to 0.1 clocks less
and an instruction fill 0.02 to 0.7 less (1.6 beside 4-pixel write-only
spans). Texture loads are now 3.8 / 3.55 (Load_Block: 4.0 / 3.9 for 2048
bytes, 3.65 / 3.2 for 512), everything that is neither span nor load
4.25 / 4.2, fills 12.1 / 11.7, copy 6.0 / 4.3.

2. The row for spans that blend with the colour buffer without the depth
buffer held the values of "no_zcmp", a case that still updates the depth
buffer. rdp_timing v73 measured the real thing (data fill / instruction
fill, back to back):

                           4 px        16 px        64 px        256 px
  2-cycle, textured        9.7 / 4.5   10.5 / 6.2    9.0 / 4.0    9.1 / 1.7
  1-cycle, textured        9.6 / 4.4   10.4 / 6.1   10.3 / 4.9    8.9 / 3.2
  320-pixel texture rectangles: 2-cycle 8.8 / 1.3, 1-cycle 8.8 / 3.0

(no texture, or shaded, as the 2-cycle textured row; Donkey Kong 64's two
1-cycle modes as the 1-cycle row.) The old row was 10.3 to 11.6 / 5.9 to
7.6. In the games these spans are wide: 13 to 17% of a frame of Donkey
Kong 64, 6% of Beetle Adventure Racing's second scene, and 55% (narrow)
of part 2 of the MGC 2011 demo.

Beyond 64 pixels what a fill costs depends on how closely the fills
follow each other (2-cycle blended spans of 256 pixels: 9.1 clocks back
to back, 2.4 to 3.1 at one fill every 125 to 500 CPU cycles; 1-cycle: 8.9
and 7.4 to 11.4). The back-to-back values are used, as before, except for
write-only spans beyond 64 pixels (as in patch 0029).

The share of depth-compared spans taken to pass stays 1 / overdraw of
the last frame. It is not measured (the reading of rdp_timing v67 behind
it does not hold: a list with the depth compare switched off draws another
kind of span); with it the game lists agree best.

Prediction against the console (read from DPC_PIPEBUSY) over the 78 lists
and variants of v61 to v65, nothing fitted: rms 0.62 clocks (data fill)
and 0.45 (instruction fill); the table of patch 0029 gives 0.70 / 0.70.

Results in the test harness (series v25 against v24): the MGC 2011 demo's
"@" frame +19 VI and the start of part 4 -1 VI (was +34 / +11; parts 1 to
4 within 0.2%, part 5 +1.5%); Donkey Kong 64's DKTV demo 10 / 376 / 34
frames of delta 2 / 3 / 4 and more (was 10 / 378 / 35; console 9 / 386 /
25) and +0.7% VI (was +0.8%); Beetle Adventure Racing's intro 239 / 323
frames in the two segments (was 238 / 321; console 231 / 325). rdp_timing
v61 run in the harness against the console, rms over its 29 lists: data
fill 0.55 clocks (was 0.67), instruction fill 0.48 (was 0.93).
